libperformant_isabelle_ml -- the native half of Performant_Isabelle_ML

This directory holds the C sources behind those Isabelle/ML modules that need
the operating system directly.  Today that is one: thread_cpu.c, the
per-thread CPU clocks read by library/thread_cpu.ML.  Each source file is
consumed by the ML file of the same name.

Build it once per machine: ./build with no argument lists the supported
targets; ./build TARGET compiles the library, checks it -- by running the
self-test against it, or on x86_64-windows, where the POSIX self-test (dlopen,
pthreads) cannot run, by checking statically that the linked file is a 64-bit
PE exporting tc_self, tc_read and tc_free -- and only then installs it as
./TARGET/libperformant_isabelle_ml.so (on Windows
./TARGET/performant_isabelle_ml.dll).  TARGET is Poly/ML's ML_PLATFORM with
any "_32" stripped from the architecture, e.g. x86_64-linux for both
x86_64-linux and x86_64_32-linux.

The built library is deliberately not committed (.gitignore covers ./*/), so a
git checkout builds it once per machine.  The conda package, by contrast, ships
it prebuilt: the `native` job of ../../.github/workflows/release-conda.yml runs
this script for each of the five targets on a GitHub-hosted runner of that
architecture at release time, and all five ./TARGET/ directories go into the one
noarch package -- library/thread_cpu.ML picks the directory for the running
platform, so a single artifact is correct everywhere (Isabelle ships its own
libsha1.so the same way).  Getting them past rattler-build's source copy needs
`use_gitignore: false` in conda/recipe.yaml, since .gitignore hides exactly
these directories; the recipe asserts all five are present, so a package cannot
build green without them.  A missing library, or one that will not load,
leaves Thread_CPU without a clock (library/thread_cpu.ML, WITHOUT A CLOCK);
the reason it reports names this build command.  The prebuilt Linux libraries
require glibc 2.34 or newer (Ubuntu 22.04, Debian 12, RHEL 9), where
pthread_getcpuclockid moved into libc; the macOS ones are stamped for macOS
11.0 or newer but were measured only on 14 and 15.  On an older system
./build TARGET produces a local copy with no such requirement.

What has actually been measured, per platform, is recorded in
../../ai-artifacts/THREAD_CPU_MEASUREMENTS.md; the header of each .c file states the
contract that follows from it.  The self-test re-measures the part of it that
a build can regress -- accuracy, a sleeping thread, a cross-thread read, the
handle after the thread has ended, and (asserted on macOS) that a held handle
is not reissued to new threads -- on every build except x86_64-windows, where
nothing executes the library before it ships; Windows behaviour rests on the
measurements in ../../ai-artifacts/THREAD_CPU_MEASUREMENTS.md section 4 instead.
